1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,933
Learn how a Japanese craft business could test Australia carefully, using company structure, cafe costs, local lifestyle, and customer behavior before making a large expansion decision.

2
00:00:11,933 --> 00:00:18,359
Warm-up: If a Japanese company opened in Australia, what would Australian customers notice first?

3
00:00:18,359 --> 00:00:26,621
What is more important for a new cafe: a beautiful concept, good coffee, or a good local partner?

4
00:00:26,621 --> 00:00:33,048
When should a company do a small test instead of opening a full branch?

5
00:00:33,048 --> 00:00:38,097
What parts of Japanese craft culture might feel attractive in Australia?

6
00:00:38,097 --> 00:00:49,112
This case has two related ideas: a cafe collaboration with a Gold Coast partner, and a possible Australian presence for a Japanese roof-tile company.

7
00:00:49,112 --> 00:00:55,079
Both ideas can share one story: Japanese craftsmanship, clay, fire, texture, and design.

8
00:00:55,079 --> 00:00:59,210
But they need separate budgets, contracts, licences, and plans.

9
00:00:59,210 --> 00:01:13,899
The best first question is not, "How do we open a company?" The better first question is, "What will the Australian business actually do?" "Opening in Australia" can mean several business models.

10
00:01:13,899 --> 00:01:23,537
A partner-led pilot means the Australian partner runs a small test, such as a pop-up, market stall, or shared cafe event.

11
00:01:23,537 --> 00:01:28,587
The Japanese company provides the story, recipes, standards, and design direction.

12
00:01:28,587 --> 00:01:34,554
An Australian subsidiary is a separate Australian company, often a Pty Ltd company.

13
00:01:34,554 --> 00:01:39,144
This may help with employees, leases, banking, and long-term growth.

14
00:01:39,144 --> 00:01:41,898
A registered foreign branch is different.

15
00:01:41,898 --> 00:01:48,324
In that model, the Japanese company itself registers and carries on business in Australia.

16
00:01:48,324 --> 00:01:54,291
A foreign company must appoint a local agent and meet Australian reporting duties.

17
00:01:54,291 --> 00:01:57,045
A joint venture is another possibility.

18
00:01:57,045 --> 00:02:04,389
In a joint venture, the Japanese side and Australian partner share money, work, risk, and control.

19
00:02:04,389 --> 00:02:09,897
For the first step, a partner-led pilot is probably the most realistic.

20
00:02:09,897 --> 00:02:12,651
But the contract must be clear.

21
00:02:12,651 --> 00:02:21,372
Calling something a "pilot" does not automatically avoid franchise-law risk if the local operator uses the brand and system.

22
00:02:21,372 --> 00:02:27,798
A common permanent structure is an Australian proprietary limited company, written as Pty Ltd.

23
00:02:27,798 --> 00:02:30,551
It is a separate legal entity.

24
00:02:30,551 --> 00:02:36,519
A Japanese parent company can own it alone or with an Australian partner.

25
00:02:36,519 --> 00:02:41,109
Before registration, the owners must decide who controls the company.

26
00:02:41,109 --> 00:02:47,076
Will the Japanese company own 100%? Will the Gold Coast partner own shares?

27
00:02:47,076 --> 00:02:52,125
Who owns the brand, customer data, recipes, and new Australian products?

28
00:02:52,125 --> 00:02:58,092
An Australian proprietary company needs at least one director who lives in Australia.

29
00:02:58,092 --> 00:03:06,354
Directors have real legal duties, so the local director should not be treated as only a contact person.

30
00:03:06,354 --> 00:03:10,485
Directors must apply for a director ID before appointment.

31
00:03:10,485 --> 00:03:18,288
The Business Registration Service can register a company, apply for a company name, and get an ACN.

32
00:03:18,288 --> 00:03:20,583
The form may be quick.

33
00:03:20,583 --> 00:03:25,632
The hard work is deciding ownership, tax, banking, and local responsibility.

34
00:03:25,632 --> 00:03:33,435
As of 19 June 2026, ASIC listed A$611 as the fee to register a standard proprietary company.

35
00:03:33,435 --> 00:03:39,861
Annual fees also apply, and the amount must be rechecked on the filing date.

36
00:03:39,861 --> 00:03:47,664
After registration, the business may need an ACN, ABN, TFN, and PAYG withholding if it pays employees.

37
00:03:47,664 --> 00:03:50,877
GST is also important. GST is 10%.

38
00:03:50,877 --> 00:03:59,598
A business generally must register for GST if its GST turnover reaches A$75,000 or more in a 12-month period.

39
00:03:59,598 --> 00:04:01,893
This is sales, not profit.

40
00:04:01,893 --> 00:04:06,483
A busy cafe can reach this level faster than expected.

41
00:04:06,483 --> 00:04:15,663
Company tax is usually 30%, but some companies can qualify for the lower 25% rate if they meet base-rate-entity rules.

42
00:04:15,663 --> 00:04:20,711
A tax adviser should check this, especially with a foreign parent.

43
00:04:20,711 --> 00:04:23,466
Employment costs are a major issue.

44
00:04:23,466 --> 00:04:33,564
From 1 July 2026, the National Minimum Wage will be A$26.44 per hour, but cafe employees may be covered by an award.

45
00:04:33,564 --> 00:04:40,908
Weekends, public holidays, casual loading, leave, workers' compensation, and payroll systems all affect real labour cost.

46
00:04:40,908 --> 00:04:43,203
Employers must also pay superannuation.

47
00:04:43,203 --> 00:04:49,629
The super guarantee rate is 12%, and Payday Super begins from 1 July 2026.

48
00:04:49,629 --> 00:04:55,137
A small cafe pilot should not start with a full brunch menu.

49
00:04:55,137 --> 00:04:58,809
The menu changes the licence and food-safety risk.

50
00:04:58,809 --> 00:05:06,153
On the Gold Coast, a business usually needs a licence to handle or prepare unpackaged food.

51
00:05:06,153 --> 00:05:13,497
Some low-risk sales, such as pre-packaged food, whole fruit, tea, and coffee, may be treated differently.

52
00:05:13,497 --> 00:05:17,627
Brisbane also requires licences for many unpackaged food businesses.

53
00:05:17,627 --> 00:05:29,103
This means a simple pilot is very different from a full cafe with eggs, dairy meals, fresh sandwiches, meat, cream cakes, or cooked brunch plates.

54
00:05:29,103 --> 00:05:38,283
Unpackaged ready-to-eat food can bring extra duties, including training, a food safety supervisor, and records under Food Safety Standard 3.2.2A.

55
00:05:38,283 --> 00:05:50,676
For the first test, keep the menu small: charcoal latte, iced charcoal latte, regular espresso drinks, one Japanese-inspired toast, one simple sweet product, and one seasonal special.

56
00:05:50,676 --> 00:05:55,266
The company should avoid unsupported health claims about edible charcoal.

57
00:05:55,266 --> 00:06:03,069
Sell the product through appearance, flavour, craft story, and experience, not as a detox or medical product.

58
00:06:03,069 --> 00:06:07,199
Australian cafe culture is often relaxed, social, and outdoor-friendly.

59
00:06:07,199 --> 00:06:12,248
Customers may dress casually, sit outside, and still expect high quality.

60
00:06:12,248 --> 00:06:16,379
The cafe should feel special, but not too formal.

61
00:06:16,379 --> 00:06:24,641
A better feeling is casual premium: excellent coffee, friendly explanations, clear prices, warm service, and an open space.

62
00:06:24,641 --> 00:06:26,936
Australia is also very multicultural.

63
00:06:26,936 --> 00:06:37,952
The 2021 Census found that 27.6% of Australians were born overseas, and the Gold Coast says almost one in three residents were born overseas.

64
00:06:37,952 --> 00:06:42,542
This helps international ideas, but "Japanese" alone is not enough.

65
00:06:42,542 --> 00:06:49,427
The stronger position is more specific: a contemporary Japanese cafe by a real roof-tile maker.

66
00:06:49,427 --> 00:06:52,640
Australia already has a strong coffee culture.

67
00:06:52,640 --> 00:06:59,066
Many customers understand flat whites, long blacks, cold brew, milk texture, and alternative milks.

68
00:06:59,066 --> 00:07:04,574
A charcoal latte may create the first purchase because it looks unusual.

69
00:07:04,574 --> 00:07:09,164
Taste and consistency create the second purchase. Convenience matters too.

70
00:07:09,164 --> 00:07:19,721
Reserve Bank of Australia research shows cards are the most widely used payment method, debit cards are common, and mobile-wallet use keeps growing.

71
00:07:19,721 --> 00:07:29,360
Cash has not disappeared, so prepare tap-and-go payments, phone wallets, clear receipts, visible surcharge information, and cash as an extra option.

72
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:32,573
The brand story can have three levels.

73
00:07:32,573 --> 00:07:44,048
Five seconds: "A Japanese craft cafe inspired by traditional roof tiles." Thirty seconds: explain the kawara business, clay and fire, and the smoked-silver latte idea.

74
00:07:44,048 --> 00:07:48,638
Deeper explanation: kiln, ibushi-gin, craft history, design, and company story.

75
00:07:48,638 --> 00:07:54,605
Brisbane and the Gold Coast should not be judged only by city name.

76
00:07:54,605 --> 00:08:00,113
The exact suburb, rent, foot traffic, partner, and customer group matter more.

77
00:08:00,113 --> 00:08:04,244
Brisbane is a larger resident, worker, and business market.

78
00:08:04,244 --> 00:08:12,047
Its official economic profile listed about 1.36 million residents, 958,000 jobs, and almost 146,000 businesses in 2024-25.

79
00:08:12,047 --> 00:08:18,014
It may be better for weekday customers, architects, developers, and later roof-tile relationships.

80
00:08:18,014 --> 00:08:25,817
The Gold Coast is Australia's sixth-largest city and has one of the fastest-growing economies in the country.

81
00:08:25,817 --> 00:08:30,866
It has strong lifestyle, tourism, beach, construction, hospitality, and visual-product markets.

82
00:08:30,866 --> 00:08:42,341
For this case, it may be better for the first cafe pilot because the partner is already there and the charcoal latte is highly visual.

83
00:08:42,341 --> 00:08:47,390
Strategically, the first cafe test could happen on the Gold Coast.

84
00:08:47,390 --> 00:08:52,439
A later permanent business or showroom relationship may fit Brisbane better.

85
00:08:52,439 --> 00:08:57,488
Selling kawara roof tiles is not the same as selling coffee.

86
00:08:57,488 --> 00:09:01,160
The roof-tile side needs technical and construction proof.

87
00:09:01,160 --> 00:09:12,176
The company may need to check product standards, wind-load requirements, installation methods, warranties, insurance, shipping weight, customs classification, installer training, and Queensland contractor licensing.

88
00:09:12,176 --> 00:09:17,684
In Queensland, a company doing roof-tiling work needs the right QBCC licence.

89
00:09:17,684 --> 00:09:24,110
A company licence needs a qualified company nominee in the same scope of work.

90
00:09:24,110 --> 00:09:30,077
This person helps supervise work and make sure it meets standards and codes.

91
00:09:30,077 --> 00:09:38,798
Ceramic roofing tiles are listed under Australian Border Force tariff classification 6905.10.00, with a general rate shown as 5%.

92
00:09:38,798 --> 00:09:45,224
Preferential treatment may apply, so a customs broker should check the product and documents.

93
00:09:45,224 --> 00:10:00,371
The first roof-tile opportunity should be smaller than a full roof project: a cafe entrance, small demonstration roof, decorative wall, restaurant design feature, architect sample display, or collaboration with a licensed Australian roofer.

94
00:10:00,371 --> 00:10:04,961
The safest sequence is preparation, controlled pilot, then permanent decision.

95
00:10:04,961 --> 00:10:14,600
In preparation, confirm brand ownership, trade-mark availability, partner role, food requirements, menu standards, product cost, labour cost, and English brand story.

96
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:21,027
In the controlled pilot, run several pop-ups or market events, not only one day.

97
00:10:21,027 --> 00:10:34,338
Measure customers per hour, purchase rate, average order value, product sales, hot versus iced sales, labour minutes per order, waste, waiting time, customer questions, and intention to buy again.

98
00:10:34,338 --> 00:10:39,387
Before the test starts, both sides should agree on success criteria.

99
00:10:39,387 --> 00:11:04,173
Otherwise, one partner may say, "Many people came, so it worked," while the other says, "Not enough people bought twice, so it failed." Only after the pilot should they decide whether to continue events, open a stall, create an Australian company, form a joint venture, open a full cafe, or begin a roof-tile demonstration.

100
00:11:04,173 --> 00:11:10,139
QUESTION 1: Why should the cafe plan and roof-tile plan be treated separately?

101
00:11:10,139 --> 00:11:18,860
ANSWER 1: They may share the same brand story, but they need different budgets, contracts, licences, and business plans.

102
00:11:18,860 --> 00:11:25,745
DISCUSSION 1: Which side sounds easier to test first: the cafe or the roof tiles?

103
00:11:25,745 --> 00:11:31,254
QUESTION 2: What is the most realistic first model for this case?

104
00:11:31,254 --> 00:11:36,762
ANSWER 2: A partner-led pilot is probably the most realistic first model.

105
00:11:36,762 --> 00:11:40,893
DISCUSSION 2: What should the first agreement make clear?

106
00:11:40,893 --> 00:11:45,024
QUESTION 3: Why is the local director role serious?

107
00:11:45,024 --> 00:11:50,532
ANSWER 3: The director has legal duties and responsibility for company compliance.

108
00:11:50,532 --> 00:11:58,335
DISCUSSION 3: Should the Gold Coast partner become a director, a manager, or only a pilot partner?

109
00:11:58,335 --> 00:12:03,843
QUESTION 4: Why can a busy weekend cafe still have high risk?

110
00:12:03,843 --> 00:12:13,941
ANSWER 4: Weekend sales can be strong, but weekend labour costs, penalty rates, super, and other employer costs can also be high.

111
00:12:13,941 --> 00:12:19,449
DISCUSSION 4: Which cost would you check first before opening a cafe?

112
00:12:19,449 --> 00:12:24,039
QUESTION 5: Why should the first cafe menu be small?

113
00:12:24,039 --> 00:12:30,465
ANSWER 5: A small menu reduces licence, food-safety, equipment, waste, training, and consistency risks.

114
00:12:30,465 --> 00:12:36,891
DISCUSSION 5: Which first menu item would be most useful for testing Australian customers?

115
00:12:36,891 --> 00:12:41,481
QUESTION 6: What does "casual premium" mean in this lesson?

116
00:12:41,481 --> 00:12:50,661
ANSWER 6: It means customers may pay for quality, but they still want relaxed service and an easy social atmosphere.

117
00:12:50,661 --> 00:12:56,169
DISCUSSION 6: How would you explain the cafe in one short sentence?

118
00:12:56,169 --> 00:13:02,136
QUESTION 7: Why might Gold Coast be better for the first cafe pilot?

119
00:13:02,136 --> 00:13:13,611
ANSWER 7: The partner is already there, the lifestyle market fits a visual cafe product, and the pilot can test demand before a permanent branch.

120
00:13:13,611 --> 00:13:19,119
DISCUSSION 7: Which city sounds better for the first test, and why?

121
00:13:19,119 --> 00:13:25,086
QUESTION 8: Why should the roof-tile plan start smaller than full roof installation?

122
00:13:25,086 --> 00:13:31,971
ANSWER 8: Full roof installation needs local licences, standards, technical proof, insurance, and installation partners.

123
00:13:31,971 --> 00:13:36,102
DISCUSSION 8: What is the safest first roof-tile demonstration?

124
00:13:36,102 --> 00:13:42,069
QUESTION 9: Why should both sides agree on success criteria before the pilot?

125
00:13:42,069 --> 00:13:47,577
ANSWER 9: Without success criteria, partners may interpret the same results differently.

126
00:13:47,577 --> 00:13:52,626
DISCUSSION 9: What result would make you continue the Australia plan?

127
00:13:52,626 --> 00:13:54,462
Speaking Practice Questions 1.

128
00:13:54,462 --> 00:14:01,806
What is the first business model you would choose: partner-led pilot, subsidiary, branch, or joint venture?

129
00:14:01,806 --> 00:14:07,314
2. What parts of the Japanese brand story should never change? 3.

130
00:14:07,314 --> 00:14:11,445
What parts should be adapted for Australian customers? 4.

131
00:14:11,445 --> 00:14:17,412
What would be your top three numbers to measure during the pilot? 5.

132
00:14:17,412 --> 00:14:23,379
What should be kept separate between the cafe plan and the roof-tile plan?

133
00:14:23,379 --> 00:14:43,575
The best Australian expansion plan is not "open everything now." A stronger plan is "test one clear thing, measure it well, then decide." For the cafe, the Gold Coast partner can help test demand, but the contract must protect the brand and explain roles.

134
00:14:43,575 --> 00:14:51,378
For roof tiles, the company needs a separate technical plan, with local building knowledge and licensed partners.

135
00:14:51,378 --> 00:15:01,476
Australia may like the idea because it combines international culture, casual cafe life, strong coffee expectations, outdoor lifestyle, and authentic design stories.

136
00:15:01,476 --> 00:15:04,689
But a good story is not enough.

137
00:15:04,689 --> 00:15:14,327
The product must be delicious, the operation practical, the legal structure clear, and the pilot strong enough to show repeat demand.
